.TH DEV.SH 1 "November 2026" "USDX Song Editor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dev.sh \- guarded build and test entry point for the USDX Song Editor workspace
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scripts/dev.sh
.I command
.RB [ options ]
.br
.B scripts/dev.sh check
.RB [ --release ]
.br
.B scripts/dev.sh test
.RB [ crate ]
.RB [ filter ]
.br
.B scripts/dev.sh build
.RB [ --release ]
.br
.B scripts/dev.sh run
.B --
.RB [ app\ arguments ]
.br
.B scripts/dev.sh corpus
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scripts/dev.sh
is the development entry point of the USDX Song Editor. It wraps cargo with a memory
guard so a build or test run cannot take the desktop down on a machine that is shared
with other workloads.
.PP
Before every run it:
.IP \(bu 2
checks the available memory (\fIMemAvailable\fP in \fI/proc/meminfo\fP) and refuses to
start below 4 GiB;
.IP \(bu 2)
caps cargo's build parallelism to \fImax(1, min(nproc / 2, available GiB / 2))\fP by
exporting \fICARGO_BUILD_JOBS\fP;
.IP \(bu 2)
prepends \fI~/.cargo/bin\fP to \fIPATH\fP so cargo is found in plain shells.
.PP
Cargo itself runs inside a systemd user scope
(\fBsystemd-run --user --scope -q -p "MemoryMax=..."\fP) limited to 70 percent of the
currently available memory, rounded down to whole MiB. Where \fBsystemd-run\fP is not
available the command runs directly.
.PP
An out-of-memory kill (exit status 137) is reported on stderr as
"killed: out of memory (the memory guard stopped the job; this is not a test failure)" -
it is never mistaken for a failing test or build. The script is written in bash with
\fBset -euo pipefail\fP and stops on the first failing step. The guard functions
(\fBmem_available_kib\fP, \fBmem_ok\fP, \fBjobs_for\fP, \fBmemory_max\fP,
\fBrun_guarded\fP) are written so they can be sourced and are tested by
\fIscripts/tests/dev_guard_test.sh\fP.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B check
.RS
The full quality gate, in order:
.IP 1. 3
\fBcargo fmt --all --check\fP (formatting must be clean),
.IP 2. 3
\fBcargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\fP (no warnings allowed),
.IP 3. 3
\fBcargo test --workspace\fP.
.RE
.IP
With \fB--release\fP the clippy and test steps run in release mode and the test step
also executes the benchmark-style tests that are normally marked \fB#[ignore]\fP
(\fB--include-ignored\fP). Ignored tests that need external data skip on their own when
their environment variable is not set.
.TP
.B test \fR[\fIcrate\fR]\fR [\fIfilter\fR]
.B cargo test
for the whole workspace, or for one crate when a crate name is given (\fB-p\fP
\fIcrate\fP). Any further arguments are passed to cargo (a test-name filter).
.TP
.B build
.RS
\fBcargo build --workspace\fP, optionally \fB--release\fP.
.RE
.TP
.B run \fB--\fR \fR[\fIapp arguments\fR]
\fBcargo run\fP. Arguments after \fB--\fP are handed to the application (for example a
song path). Note that plain \fBcargo\fP accepts no \fB--workspace\fP flag here, so
\fBrun\fP always runs the single binary crate.
.TP
.B corpus
Runs the opt-in real-song round-trip test
(\fBcargo test -p usdx-format --test corpus -- --ignored\fP). It sets
\fBUSDX_CORPUS\fP from the environment, defaulting to the approved USDX Song Factory
output folders (the \fInew\fP, \fIrepaired/recreated\fP and \fIrepaired/sync\fP
folders of that repository). See \fIPlan/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md\fP section 9 for the
folder rules; the songs are read-only, tests never write into them.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DEV_MEMINFO
Path of the meminfo file the guard reads instead of \fI/proc/meminfo\fP. Used by the
guard tests so they can exercise the 4 GiB floor and the job calculation without
touching the real system.
.TP
.B DEV_NPROC
Overrides the CPU count the job cap is computed from (the value of \fBnproc\fP).
.TP
.B USDX_CORPUS
Colon-separated list of folders the corpus test walks. Only files that contain a
\fB#BPM\fP header are treated as songs; the Factory helper files (\fIlyrics.txt\fP,
\fIlanguage.txt\fP, \fIsong_end.txt\fP, \fIanchors.txt\fP) and every \fI.bak\fP file
are skipped. Set automatically by \fBcorpus\fP when unset.
.TP
.B CARGO_BUILD_JOBS
Exported by the script for every cargo run; do not set it yourself, the guard computes
it. See \fBjobs_for\fP in the DESCRIPTION.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All requested steps succeeded.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: no or unknown subcommand.
.TP
.B 1
The memory guard refused to start, a build or test step failed, or the meminfo source
could not be read.
.TP
.B 137
The job was killed by the memory limit. The script reports this on stderr as an
out-of-memory kill and passes the status through; it is not a test failure.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I scripts/dev.sh
The script itself.
.TP
.I scripts/tests/dev_guard_test.sh
Tests for the guard functions (sourced, not executed).
.TP
.I /proc/meminfo
Default source of the \fIMemAvailable\fP value; overridable with \fBDEV_MEMINFO\fP.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cargo (1),
.BR systemd-run (1)
.PP
\fIDocs/building.md\fP - how to build and test the project,
\fIPlan/IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md\fP section 2.5 - the specification of the script.
